ect so  uh the foundation uh is all geared up to  ensure the  ensure that the internet remains as  public as possible and open to all  essentially  to summarize what the quote is and how  the common voice engages with that in  particular is through building  trustworthy ai and enabling open data  sets  so let's talk about the problem at hand  and what we're looking to solve with  common voice  specifically the problem relates to  technologies that leverage ai models and  the inherent biases that they uh are  trained with so simply put if a data set  doesn't have representation of diverse  groups uh the models will not perform  well for set groups it kind of makes  sense that way  uh and speech technology in particular  is no exception  the performance of automatic speech  recognition is often poor for less  represented groups and this problem has  become more and more visible as the use  of voice-operated devices continues to  grow around the globe  people whose speech patterns differ from  societal norms find that their devices  you know just do not understand them  particularly well or even worse yet  their languages aren't supported at all  some examples of this can be seen in  some english language training sets  which  may skew heavily into direction towards  male demographics as is seen in google  speech recognition software which is 13  more accurate for men than for women  so with all that being said what is  common voice and how does it aim to  solve helps up with problems  well let's just  uh get some context first into the  project and how it was formed uh it  originally started in 2017 and it was  launched in a completely english only uh  as a as a completely english-only data  set  and helped power deep speech  from then its populated popularity grew  and so did its language set which  included uh  german irish and many others uh as it  continued to grow forward  uh and that's been fantastic then there  was a restructure at mozilla itself and  then that's how the mozilla foundation  kind of took ownership of the project  and as a result of that uh to today  we've you know grown the project even  more and  with help of many partners around the  globe uh we continue to hope to grow the  project  so how does it all come together in the  real world like how do we actually  create data sets and power this  application uh well it really comes from  uh the countless volunteers that donate  their time and voice to the project uh  generally said if a  member of the community wants to add uh  language to the platform there's a  little bit of a procedure for them to  follow and that includes uh obviously  making the request as well as then  facilitating uh translation of the  website into that language because  obviously people who speak the language  would like to interact with our platform  in their native language it makes sense  and then after collecting enough open  sentences for people to read uh that new  language will launch into the platform  and then we'll welcome other volunteers  to donate their time and their uh  voices to help facilitate  click generation and after all that on a  quarterly release cycle we transformed  the raw data  by cleaning and  anonymizing some of the data and then  release it publicly to the  to the world to use  now let's uh quickly walk through the  platform itself and give you guys a  better understanding of how exactly this  comes to be  so  to start off with we give people an  option uh to sign up and create a  profile and then attach certain metadata  to the profile that will be attached to  the clips that they're validating and  speaking however this is completely  optional you do not need an account to  get started  uh the main component of the application  is the ability to donate your voice by  reading uh sentences that are given to  you and then  having those  clips validated by other community  members and you can contribute either  either which way or both or however you  want so you don't even need a microphone  to actually help the platform you could  start by validating clips and as you  grow more comfortable you could actually  donate your voice as well  and then  uh the whole purpose of the the platform  in and of itself is to allow  and build open data sets for other  organizations to implement and use in  their speech technologies so of course  we offer that ability to download the  the data and it's it's available under a  cc-0 license so pretty accessible and  there's just a few little like privacy  check marks you have to sign saying like  you don't want to expose people's  identities and stuff like that but all  in all it's very uh open for you to use  uh however which way you like  so let's talk about the data set  specifically uh after downloading the  data you'll  realize there's a lot of information  associated with the clips and generally  that  it's organized in tsp files uh  so the tsp files hold all the metadata  surrounding the clips and there we  separated the contents of clips into  these files based on a certain criteria  of if it's valid if it's been validated  if it's been invalidated and if we don't  really know exactly where uh it sits in  uh in that in that sphere  so generally uh the validated uh list  contains clips that have received more  than two  validations and have more valid upvotes  than downloads so people in the  community have generally regarded it as  a valid clip whereas invalidated tsp  files contain rows of clips that have  been  generally  engaged with with downloads people don't  really  agree with the clip representing the  sentence that it's supposed to read  okay  so that was a quick uh hot take of the  data sets in and of itself but uh we're  actually on the precipice of releasing  the next data set of version eight and  it's looking like a great one uh to  speak candidly  uh it'll have over 18 000 hours of voice  data which is pretty fantastic and it  has over 450 000  uh people and volunteers who have both  recorded and validated clips so it's a  truly international global effort that  uh this even exists  so obviously uh we'd like to take a step  back and look at the impact of our of  our data sets and how it's impacted the  real world and uh  what good it's been able to accomplish  one quick example uh recently has been  the adoption of the rwanda community's  use of uh common voice data to power a  covey tool that allows individuals to  interact with the platform using their  native languages as well as french and  english english  and from an academic standpoint uh we  continue to see use of our data sets uh  throughout academia and corporate  research  and then looking forward into the future  of common voice because i mean  you also have to look forward to the  future um  we're actually experimenting and looking  to adapt more uh customizable accents  and variants for clips so  not every speaker can fall into one  category of accent and we want to really  diversify the actions and then also give  more context for each clip  as well as the actual data set  enhancements themselves uh one of the  things i would like to call out as well  is the increased frequency of data being  released so ideally we're going to start  pushing out more and more data releases  yearly which will allow people to engage  with the data  much fresher data that is  and generally uh the goals for the  foundation are even more broad and that  we truly want to engage with the  community in multiple different avenues  including you know  uh enabling organizations to use the  data sets to  incorporating more diverse data sets etc  etc  and yeah that was uh my quick  take on  the common voice team and uh where we're  at right now and i  hope that you all enjoyed the talk was  very quick again and there's a lot more  to be learned again on the platform  itself at our github and discourse and  if you're feeling uh motivated please  feel free to contribute back to the  platform by donating your voice or even  just validating some clips  so yeah thanks for your time and thanks  for listening  all right uh thank you very much for the  great job gabrielle  we have a question so  dan is asking will danish be included in  the commonwealth 8 data set  and  i would have to double check to see if  the specific language is being included  uh i haven't done so though so i can't  say yes or no right now  okay  you don't have any more you'll be able  to  see the data set released sometime next  week  okay  now if you have any other questions feel  free to post those in the chats  and if you uh don't have questions now  but come up with questions later feel  free to again engage with us on our  discourse  okay there's a question from uh patrick  uh patrick is asking does common voice  analyzes somehow how biased a language  is example male versus female speakers  well i don't think we as like a platform  uh  skew the data being released in any  capacity like that but we provide the  metadata associated with eclipse that  allows people using the data to analyze  for gender or age for example if it's  available on the clip so thus you could  kind of tell which which way it's skewed  i  suppose uh there's another question is  there a demo for using a pi torch module  such as back to vector  uh yes jonathan i think you can find one  on the hugging face hub on on on the  spaces i'm sure  there's going to be at least one uh one  such demo  all right the  next question is uh is there any overlap  between commonwealth versions  so do you mean like uh the data sets  being backwards compatible i suppose  uh i don't think there's any guarantee  that the major versions between common  voice releases are backwards compatible  but generally i think there are release  notes that are associated if if that is  the case or otherwise  okay thank you  the next question is from felix and  felix is asking how are the accents  going to be better represented in the  future  and what efforts are made there  so we've actually enabled the ability  for individuals to add their own accents  so that we don't have to quantify or  qualify what accents there are and  really allow the community to drive uh  what they think makes sense about their  specific language so the idea is that  any individual can can  confidently say that i belong to one  accent and then let the community as a  whole pick up on that  okay and  the next question is how do you select  the sentences to read that's a fantastic  question so there is a whole  platform side of things where  there's two kind of avenues for sentence  selecting  uh one is we engage with partners that  allow us to  pull in open source sentences that we  can add to our platform and have uh  people read and validate so for example  wikipedia  articles and and such  otherwise they're added individually by  contributors who've you know ensured  that the sentences that they're adding  are you know appropriate and open source  but also they can contribute  individually  okay there's a  follow-up question is it possible to  suggest some sentences  yes i do believe that through the  sentence platform uh you can actually  suggest  be added to the corpus  okay nice uh we have two more questions  so uh dan is asking uh do you have plans  to include dialects as well uh rather  than only accents  that's a good question i'm not too  certain if that's been explored just yet  the ability to add dialects but i think  that's something we could definitely  think about in the future  thank you and  patrick is asking  how do you know that a sample that has  been apported twice is actually of a  good quality  yeah so that's a general heuristic we've  added to help validate the  the clips themselves i mean obviously  it's not a perfect science and there  could be  um  you know an anomaly where multiple  people validate a bad clip but that's a  result of uh you know open source data  sets like this and platforms like this  it all kind of revolves around the  quality and  strength of the open source community at  large  okay  oh we have another question  uh julian is asking how do you decide or  prioritize new languages and anything we  can do to help you have as many  contributors as possible  yeah so actually a lot of the  idea of like bringing in new languages  to the platform comes from the community  itself and  us in the foundation don't really have  too much say in it so if it's a  community driven  addition then it's likely to succeed by  you naturally over time gathering uh  supporters and contributors to that  language  and as for like adding or helping by  um have as many contributors as possible  i mean just engaging with the platform  engaging with the discourse and  community at large as well as obviously  you know sharing on social media that  you're taking part of this platform and  such  all goes  to help us and the community  thank you  and uh yeah another question  is asking is there a plan to make common  voice have more natural conversational  or spoken speech instead of reading  speech  i think naturally as the data sets  continue to grow in senses as we collect  become more and more diverse that'll  just naturally occur over time but i  don't think there's a direct focus on  uh engaging with like strictly uh more  conversational speech verbs  sentences that we pull from wikipedia  for example  we still have a few more minutes left so  if you have another any other questions  just click  post those in the comments  all right uh we  uh have no more questions uh thanks a  lot gabriel uh for the  great talk  and uh thank you all for uh joining  joining this talk and uh i'll see you up  in half an hour for the for the next  talk so good day or good evening thank  you thanks for having me and uh hope  everybody enjoyed the talk and hope to  see you guys on the discourse and uh  engaging with the platform have a great  day  thank you

